I wrote the round-3 plan for seed 804. The check (scripts/c06_check.py 804 3) passes with exit code 0. I also appended a Round 3 section to /Users/wxy/Desktop/Partura/research/coldstart_C06/partura/s804/notebook.md: beliefs, prior predictions for each task, and a check of the round-2 predictions.

**Slot a (E), core day_centroid(sqrt(Volume)).** Head +1.39 is the cleanest and most out-of-sample-robust source in the library. Its only periphery so far is smoothing plus neg. The slot has 13 seeds in four groups, each a test that can fail:
- names with limit-lock or frozen days are screened out;
- a cross-day ts_corr of the centroid with the open-to-close return tests whether back-loaded volume hurts because it pushes the close up;
- the centroid against its own 120/240-day baseline separates the persistent trait from short-term shifts;
- a pool restricted to names with little closing-auction trading tests the passive-fund explanation.

**Slot b (N), where volume trades on the intraday price path.** The idea is that buying at local intraday highs is retail chasing that later reverses. The main seeds correlate minute price, measured against its 30/60-minute rolling mean, with sqrt volume. A div(M,M) NaN mask removes the opening and closing auction minutes. Together these strip out the "day direction x volume timing" overlap with the centroid family. I checked on a test tensor that the mask is NaN at minutes 0 and 238-240. Further seeds are VWAP minus TWAP and the price of the highest-impact minutes, both divided by minute volatility. Two raw day_corr(Close, Volume) seeds stay in as references. 13 seeds.

**R, one group with one variant.** It replaces head +1.39's single measurement with an equal-weight rank average of the centroid and the late-session share (threshold +1.491). Its correlation with the known family is estimated at 0.46-0.49, close to the 0.5 limit; above 0.5 the group is void.

Every expression parses. I left control-bucket ranking out on purpose: at about 0.85 with head +1.39, the table and the library ban would remove it. I also left out bucketing on LimitWidth or BoardCode, because their heavy ties hit trap 2. The NBER number (w24014) for Chen-Gao-He-Jiang-Xiong and the citation details for Kakushadze, Barber-Odean and Campbell-Grossman-Wang were confirmed by web search. The Kyle 1985 DOI was not looked up.
